Continuing with the previous posts on Rate limiting algorithms, let's check out the implementation of the Token Bucket Rate Limiting algorithm. It works by filling a bucket with tokens at a fixed rate (e.g., 1 token/sec). Each request consumes a token, and if no tokens are available, the request is rejected. The bucket has a maximum capacity, so it can handle bursts of traffic as long as the burst doesn’t exceed the number of tokens in the bucket.

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽𝘀 𝘁𝗼 𝗶𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁:

1. Define a Token Refill Rate - Set a rate at which tokens are added to the bucket, such as 1 token/sec.

2. Track Token Consumption - For each incoming request, deduct 1 token from the bucket.

3. Refill Tokens - Continuously refill the bucket at the defined rate, up to its max capacity, ensuring unused tokens can accumulate for future bursts.

4. Rate Limit Check - Check if there are enough tokens in the bucket. If the bucket is empty, reject the request until tokens are replenished.

