𝗝𝗲𝗱𝗶𝘀 is a popular Java library used to interact with Redis and I've used it for implementing our Rate Limiter because it provides a simple and intuitive API for executing Redis commands from JVM applications.

The first step is to store each client’s token count and last refill time in Redis using unique keys. 

Next, retrieve the current token count and the last refill time to calculate how many tokens should be added based on the time elapsed since the last refill.

Now compare the current token count to determine if the request can be allowed. If tokens are available, deduct one token, else, block the request.

Finally, I updated the token count and last refill time in Redis.

